+---
+id: task-001-json-pointer-resolution-rfc-6901
+type: task
+title: "JSON Pointer resolution (RFC 6901)"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# JSON Pointer resolution (RFC 6901)
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need a standard way to refer to a value inside a JSON document. Implement JSON Pointer,
+as specified in RFC 6901. This is the first of several features that build on each other; the next
+ones will use it.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Export `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`.
+- Given a JSON document and a JSON Pointer string, return the value the pointer refers to.
+- Throw an error when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer (RFC 6901 section 3 syntax).
+- Throw an error when the pointer does not resolve to a value of the document.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- The empty pointer `""` refers to the whole document.
+- Each reference token is preceded by `/`; a non-empty pointer not starting with `/` is invalid.
+- Escape sequences are decoded in this order: `~1` becomes `/`, then `~0` becomes `~`. Any other `~`
+  sequence is invalid.
+- Array indices: a token is valid only as `0` or a non-zero-leading decimal integer (no `+`, no
+  leading zeros, no `-` for resolution). Out-of-range or non-numeric index on an array is an error.
+- Object members are looked up by exact key; a missing member is an error.
+- Traversing into a primitive (string, number, boolean, null) is an error.
+- Tokens are matched against the document as given: no coercion of the pointer into other types.
+- Resolution is deterministic and has no runtime dependency (the package has none).
+- Tests cover the RFC 6901 section 5 examples and the error cases above, run with `npm test`.
+
+## Requirements traceability
+
+- Feature: JSON Pointer support for the json-toolkit library.
+- Standard: RFC 6901 (JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Pointer).
+- Consumers: the other team projects that import this library (see README.md).
